import "core"


# A call whose declared result is a declaration variable, landing where nothing requires a type.
  A term may be handed on unrewritten — rewriting is a chain and the consumer's followup applies
  it — so the tree build leaves a term that already reaches its destination alone. A Top
  destination accepts everything, so it left such a term alone too, and there was no followup to
  apply it: the applied tuple itself became the consumer's runtime value, and the consumer had
  nothing to dispatch on.
  A declared result spelled by a declaration variable is exactly what produces a Top destination.
  @gidG@ returns @$_B@, which in the generic frame @useG@'s body is built in reads as Top, so
  @useG@ answered with the raw @/gidG(/x=5)@ tuple where @operator+@ wanted an Integer:
    Invalid intOp signature
  @useI@ below is the same program with those three positions spelled Integer instead. It always
  worked, and it is what says the fault was the variable rather than the shape.
gidG[$B](x -> $_B) -> $_B
gidG(x)= x

useG[$B](fn(a -> $_B) -> $_B, x -> $_B) -> $_B
useG(fn(a), x)= fn(a= /gidG(x= x))

gidI(x -> Integer) -> Integer
gidI(x)= x

useI(fn(a -> Integer) -> Integer, x -> Integer) -> Integer
useI(fn(a), x)= fn(a= /gidI(x= x))

# The same defect is what stopped a fold over a polymorphic accumulator, which is the shape it was
  found from. The recursive call is what reaches the step function, and it reached it as the
  unevaluated @/foldrG(..)@ tuple:
    Failed match in eval callTree:
      Val: TupleVal /operator+(/r = 1, /l = TupleVal /foldrG(/init = 0, ..))
      Options: [+(/r=Float, /l=Float), +(/r=Integer, /l=Integer)]
  Both the element type and the accumulator are variables here, so nothing about this fold is
  concrete until it is called.
foldrG[$A, $B](lst -> ConsList[$T -> $_A], fn(acc -> $_B, v -> $_A) -> $_B, init -> $_B) -> $_B
foldrG(lst= Nil, fn(acc, v), init)= init
foldrG(lst= Cons(head, tail), fn(acc, v), init)= fn(acc= /foldrG(lst= tail, fn(acc, v)= fn(acc= acc, v= v), init= init), v= head)

# The accumulator need not be the element type: the last fold folds a list of Integers into a
  String.
main{io -> IO} =
  #assert(test= useG(fn(a)= a + 1, x= 5) == 6)
  #assert(test= useI(fn(a)= a + 1, x= 5) == 6)
  #assert(test= foldrG(lst= [1, 2, 3], fn(acc, v)= acc + v, init= 0) == 6)
  #assert(test= foldrG(lst= [], fn(acc, v)= acc + v, init= 0) == 0)
  #assert(test= foldrG(lst= [1, 2], fn(acc, v)= acc ++ "x", init= "s") == "sxx")
  io
